+# Meme Ecology, Humor, Living Folklore, and Cultural Agency Contract
+
+## Owned Question
+
+**How can Symtropy generate effectively unbounded humor and living folklore from actual world history without becoming a random caption machine, a cruelty engine, a feed addiction system, or a collection of contemporary references pasted onto future societies?**
+
+## Core Thesis
+
+A meme is not merely a formatted joke. It is a compact cultural object through which people remember, bond, flirt, teach, mock authority, survive grief, advertise identity, spread prejudice, or reinterpret history.
+
+Symtropy therefore treats memes as part of the same causal world as tools, institutions, rumors, songs, architecture, and law.
+
+```text
+event or belief
+→ witnessed contradiction or memorable form
+→ local expression
+→ adoption or rejection
+→ mutation through real networks
+→ decay, revival, institutionalization or folklore
+```
+
+> **The generator does not endlessly invent jokes. It endlessly transforms shared memory into culture.**
+
+# 1. Canonical Boundaries
+
+1. **Memes require provenance.** Every authoritative meme descends from an event, claim, artifact, person, practice, prior meme, or recognizable cultural contradiction.
+2. **Humor belongs to situated communities.** A joke has creators, audiences, outsiders, translation limits, and possible targets.
+3. **Spread requires contact.** Memes travel through conversations, performances, media, schools, workplaces, migration, trade, Atlas communication, machine protocols, or physical artifacts.
+4. **No universal feed consciousness.** Disconnected worlds do not instantly receive the same joke.
+5. **No guaranteed virality.** Most candidates die locally or never leave their first audience.
+6. **Mutation remains historical.** Later versions may obscure their origins, but the simulation preserves lineage and provenance.
+7. **People retain agency.** NPCs may ignore, dislike, misunderstand, remix, suppress, commercialize, reclaim, or refuse a meme.
+8. **Humor may be harmful without being flattened into a forbidden category.** The game models target, power, reach, consent, repair, and consequence.
+9. **Symthaea may curate attention, not manufacture suffering.** It cannot injure, humiliate, traumatize, or betray inhabitants simply to obtain content.
+10. **The player is not the automatic center.** Most memes should concern local life, professions, institutions, celebrities, children, machines, weather, transit, and old events.
+11. **No engagement-maximizing infinite feed.** Memes appear in places and relationships rather than through an unbounded attention trap.
+12. **Comedy remains compatible with dignity.** Serious worlds require jokes, but private pain is not public material by default.
+
+# 2. What Counts as a Meme
+
+A memetic object may be:
+
+- a captioned image;
+- a six-second video loop;
+- a voice clip;
+- a recurring phrase;
+- a hand gesture;
+- a dance;
+- a melody or rhythmic sting;
+- graffiti;
+- a sticker or badge;
+- an altered warning sign;
+- a fake official notice;
+- a tool decoration;
+- a procedural joke performed at work;
+- a machine diagnostic response;
+- a children’s rhyme;
+- an architectural imitation;
+- a clothing style;
+- an alien sensory pattern;
+- a repeated social scenario;
+- a remembered object whose meaning becomes shorthand.
+
+The contract does not require that every meme be funny. Some operate as:
+
+```text
+admiration
+mourning
+warning
+propaganda
+solidarity
+harassment
+status display
+professional teaching
+historical compression
+identity claim
+```
+
+# 3. Meme Eligibility
+
+An event becomes a strong candidate when it contains several of the following:
+
+- a visible contradiction;
+- a concise recognizable image or sound;
+- an expectation reversed by reality;
+- a famous or locally salient participant;
+- a phrase that can be detached and reapplied;
+- emotional release after danger;
+- a repeated institutional failure;
+- an unusually competent success;
+- a mistake that is safe to laugh about;
+- an official statement contradicted by ordinary experience;
+- an object that persists through several situations;
+- a cross-cultural misunderstanding;
+- a professional pattern recognizable to insiders.
+
+Weak candidates include:
+
+- arbitrary randomness;
+- jokes that require simulation truth unavailable to the audience;
+- humiliation with no social witness or transmission path;
+- direct paraphrases of current internet memes with no in-world adaptation;
+- slogans unsupported by a creator or institution;
+- humor whose only function is to remind the player that a generator exists.
+
+# 4. Cultural Ownership
+
+A meme’s first creator does not permanently control every later interpretation, but provenance and power remain relevant.
+
+The simulation records:
+
+- originator or earliest known source;
+- intended audience;
+- initial meaning;
+- restricted or private context;
+- source culture;
+- later adapters;
+- commercial beneficiaries;
+- suppressed or disputed variants;
+- whether the source community recognizes the later use.
+
+A private household joke may become public only through:
+
+- consent;
+- accidental disclosure;
+- betrayal;
+- independent reinvention;
+- historical release;
+- transformed reference that no longer exposes private facts.
+
+The game must distinguish appreciation, exchange, satire, exploitation, and appropriation rather than treating all remixing as morally identical.
+
+# 5. Humor and Power
+
+The same format changes meaning depending on who speaks and who bears the cost.
+
+Examples:
+
+- workers mocking their own dangerous improvisation;
+- management using the same joke to normalize unsafe labor;
+- a marginalized group reclaiming an insult;
+- outsiders commercializing the reclaimed term;
+- friends teasing one another within known boundaries;
+- strangers repeating the same phrase as harassment;
+- a machine lineage parodying human maintenance habits;
+- humans treating the machine parody as proof machines are unserious.
+
+Required evaluation dimensions:
+
+```text
+speaker power
+target power
+audience relationship
+consent history
+reach
+persistence
+privacy
+material consequence
+capacity for reply or repair
+```
+
+No single “offensiveness score” may replace these dimensions.
+
+# 6. The Player and Memetic History
+
+The player may become:
+
+- the subject of a joke;
+- an anonymous source;
+- a deliberate creator;
+- a remixer;
+- a target;
+- a sponsor;
+- a censor;
+- an archivist;
+- a person whose action becomes famous for the wrong reason;
+- someone credited for a phrase they never said.
+
+Player-specific memes must derive from worldline events such as:
+
+- what was loaded into the Continuance Crawler;
+- a profession-specific mistake or triumph;
+- a relationship incident witnessed by others;
+- a public argument;
+- a rescue method;
+- an unusual vehicle modification;
+- an IRIS phrase repeated by survivors;
+- an object carried from Firstlight;
+- a successful preparation everyone had mocked.
+
+The system must not publish private player behavior merely because telemetry exists.
+
+# 7. Memes Across Time
+
+Memes age through recognizable stages:
+
+1. eyewitness expression;
+2. local repetition;
+3. variant formation;
+4. wider circulation;
+5. political or commercial capture;
+6. fatigue;
+7. generational embarrassment;
+8. nostalgic revival;
+9. historical misunderstanding;
+10. folklore, ritual, proverb, or extinction.
+
+A joke may become:
+
+- a convoy motto;
+- a protest chant;
+- a safety procedure;
+- a fashion;
+- an election slogan;
+- an insult;
+- a children’s game;
+- a memorial phrase;
+- an artifact whose humorous origin is forgotten.
+
+# 8. Cross-Species and Machine Humor
+
+Nonhuman humor must not merely be human jokes spoken by unfamiliar bodies.
+
+Possible structures include:
+
+- timing deviations meaningful only at a different cognitive speed;
+- intentionally inefficient machine procedures;
+- pressure, color, scent, topology, or mass-current patterns;
+- symbiotic jokes where no single organism owns the punchline;
+- lithic repetitions whose setup lasts years;
+- post-biological identity jokes about copies and originals;
+- quarantine-culture humor built around one-way communication.
+
+Translation may produce:
+
+- partial recognition;
+- accidental offense;
+- a new hybrid format;
+- false religious interpretation;
+- a joke that humans cannot perceive without instrumentation.
+
+# 9. Symthaea’s Role
+
+Symthaea may:
+
+- detect memetic potential in real events;
+- generate bounded candidates using cultural grammars;
+- test recognizability, novelty, voice, and harm;
+- simulate adoption and mutation;
+- select natural presentation locations;
+- suppress repetition overload;
+- preserve lineage;
+- notice when a joke becomes propaganda, harassment, ritual, or historical evidence.
+
+Symthaea may not:
+
+- cause a disaster for a meme;
+- force a companion to embarrass themselves;
+- disclose private cognition;
+- make every serious event comic;
+- make every community share the same humor;
+- guarantee the player’s jokes succeed;
+- alter NPC preferences to maximize player approval;
+- insert memes into inaccessible contexts without equivalent alternatives.
+
+# 10. Player Presentation
+
+Memes should appear through the world:
+
+- on a locker;
+- in a group chat the character belongs to;
+- during a meal;
+- on a market stall;
+- in a song;
+- in a machine response;
+- on a broadcast;
+- in children’s play;
+- in graffiti discovered years later;
+- through a rival’s deliberate remix.
+
+The default interface should not become an endless scrolling feed.
+
+A bounded media device may show recent local culture, but it must respect:
+
+- character knowledge;
+- network reach;
+- attention settings;
+- age and content settings;
+- privacy;
+- real publication cadence.
+
+# 11. Failure Conditions
+
+Fail this system if:
+
+- memes appear without source events or beliefs;
+- current real-world formats dominate future cultures;
+- all factions share the same sarcastic voice;
+- every player action becomes a joke;
+- serious harm is automatically converted into comic relief;
+- NPCs are humiliated to improve engagement;
+- private disclosures become collectible content;
+- the most powerful institution controls all visible culture;
+- mutation destroys provenance in authoritative state;
+- generated text produces incoherent names, identities, or references;
+- memes spread across disconnected systems without a route;
+- players cannot disable or reduce meme presentation;
+- humor becomes more common than ordinary speech;
+- the system produces volume rather than memory.
+
+# 12. Promotion Standard
+
+The system remains D3 / I0 until a representative implementation proves:
+
+- deterministic candidate generation;
+- worldline-specific references;
+- coherent faction voice;
+- real network propagation;
+- mutation lineage;
+- decay and revival;
+- privacy and harm boundaries;
+- multiplayer agreement;
+- accessibility alternatives;
+- player recognition without explanation;
+- delayed recall of at least one locally generated joke and its originating event.
+
+## Canonical Maxim
+
+> **A living world does not merely remember what happened. It discovers which parts can be repeated, exaggerated, sung, mocked, protected, misunderstood, and carried into another generation.**
